# Day 44/50 — Firewalls

Imagine a building with a security gate.

People and vehicles don't simply enter without being checked. 
There are rules about what gets through and what stays outside.

A **Firewall** does something similar for computer networks.
It monitors incoming and outgoing network traffic and allows or blocks it based on predefined security rules.
It acts as a barrier between trusted and untrusted networks, helping prevent unauthorized access.

But a firewall doesn't make a system completely secure. 
It is one layer of a much larger security approach.

And that's an interesting thought.

Protection doesn't always mean shutting everything out. Sometimes, it means knowing what to allow and what to keep away.

**Because a good boundary isn't meant to stop everything. It's meant to protect what matters.**
